  stickyNoteInfos:
  - title: 'Mesh Sample:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -705
      y: -813
      width: 379
      height: 144
    contents: 'We''re sampling the mesh to get the position and normal of the eye''s
      socket. We store that information in custom attributes to be able to use them
      later on.



      This allows us to avoid sampling the mesh in the update
      context, as it can be costly. Thanks to this, we''re sampling the mesh only
      when the particles are born.

'
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: 'Index:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -433
      y: -1028
      width: 253
      height: 170
    contents: 'The socket for each eye has been baked in the first 100 triangles
      of the mesh. As we want to spawn a thousand feathers, we''re taking the spawnIndex
      % 100 and storing it in a custom attribute.



      This is creating a per-particle
      index from 0-99 that allows us to identify which eye socket each feather is
      in.

'
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: 'Opening Animation:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -1207
      y: -447
      width: 336
      height: 174
    contents: "The opening animation is a per-particle value from 0 (close) to 1
      (open). We're using the VFX time to sample a curve and drive the flower animation.
      As we don't want each flower to open at the same time, we're offsetting the
      time thanks to our \u201CIndex\u201D attribute that has been calculated in
      the Initialize Context."
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: 'Set Position:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -1816
      y: -99
      width: 401
      height: 265
    contents: 'We want to spawn our Particles Feathers in a Circle. The circle center
      should be the Eye Socket position, and the Circle Should also be oriented in
      the Eye''s Socket Normal Direction.

'
    theme: Black
    textSize: Medium
  - title: Radius
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -481
      y: 401
      width: 399
      height: 193
    contents: "The size of each \u201Ccircle\u201D cannot be the same for each particle,
      as each eye socket is a different size.\r\nWe're modifying the radius of each
      circle based on the particle size, as in the initialize context, the particle
      size has been set based on the socket size baked in the UV.x component of our
      mesh.\r\n\r\nWe're also animating the circle radius based on the flower opening
      0-1 animation value. When the flower is closed, the circle is bigger, and when
      the flower is open, the circle radius shrinks slightly.\r\n"
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: 'Transform:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -1262
      y: 467
      width: 379
      height: 100
    contents: 'We''re changing the Z component of the transform to be able to push
      or pull the circle position based on the flower animation.

'
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: 'LookAt:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -1031
      y: -35
      width: 262
      height: 100
    contents: "The LookAt operator allows us to create a transform so that we can
      properly \u201Clocate\u201D and \u201Corient\u201D each circle on every eye's
      socket.\r\n"
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: 'Axis Direction:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -2013
      y: 819
      width: 840
      height: 346
    contents: "The \u201Cinitial direction\u201D attribute is the normal pointing
      outward of each eye's socket. The \u201CDirection\u201D attribute is set by
      the \u201CPosition\u201D arc circle block in the update context and corresponds
      to the circle's normal.\r\n\r\nTo provide the orient block with an axis, we're
      interpolating (lerping) between the initial direction and direction attribute.\r\nTo
      lerp between the two, the \u201Copen\u201D attribute value (from the Flower
      Animation) is used.\r\n"
    theme: Black
    textSize: Medium
  - title: 'Open+Noise:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -993
      y: 1739
      width: 282
      height: 145
    contents: "The lerp between the Initial Direction and Direction attribute is
      driven by the \u201Copen\u201D attribute. But to create some nice, subtle movement,
      we're adding some noise.\r\nThis creates an impression of \u201Cbreathing\u201D
      eyelashes.\r\n"
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: 'Orient Fixed Axis:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 195
      y: 2064
      width: 682
      height: 327
    contents: "Again, we're using the orient block to orient our particles. This
      time, we're using the \u201CFixed Axis\u201D mode. This mode allows us to align
      the particles along an \u201Cup\u201D vector while still facing the camera.
      \n\nIt is often used for grass on the ground or flames. This way, the particles
      are not rotating when looked at from above. \r\n"
    theme: Black
    textSize: Medium
  - title: 'ORIENT: FACE CAMERA'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 2445
      y: 600
      width: 798
      height: 417
    contents: "The Orient Block is very useful and can frequently be found in the
      output context. It is responsible for \u201Corienting\u201D the particles.\r\nDifferent
      modes are available, but the most common ones are Face Camera Position and
      Face Camera Plan, as they\u2019re very useful for billboard quad. These modes
      orient the particles so that they're facing the camera (position or plane).\r\n\r\nUnder
      the hood, this block is setting the AxisX, AxisY, AxisZ attributes that are
      used to represent particle orientation.\r\n"
    theme: Black
    textSize: Medium
  - title: 'Noise operator:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 1287
      y: -99
      width: 339
      height: 248
    contents: 'The value noise operator can be very useful to add some life or animation
      to particle attributes.



      Here we''re using the time and a random number
      (to offset the noise per particle) to sample the value of noise.

      The
      output values are then used to drive the eye''s pupils size and the eye''s
      size.

      This is what is giving noisy/flickering eyes staring at you.



      1D
      noise is cheap, but 2D & 3D noise are more expensive to compute. The cost increases
      with the number of octaves you''re using. So if possible, try to keep the dimension
      and number of octaves low.

'
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: FEATHERS
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 323
      y: -1737
      width: 433
      height: 101
    contents: 
    theme: Black
    textSize: Huge
  - title: EYES
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 2776
      y: -1711
      width: 234
      height: 101
    contents: 
    theme: Black
    textSize: Huge
  - title: BODY
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 4395
      y: -1662
      width: 252
      height: 101
    contents: 
    theme: Black
    textSize: Huge
  - title: 'Opening Animation:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 1090
      y: -545
      width: 288
      height: 160
    contents: "The opening animation is a per-particle value from 0 (close) to 1
      (open). We're using the VFX time to sample a curve and drive the flower animation.
      As we don't want each flower to open at the same time, we're offsetting the
      time thanks to our \u201CIndex\u201D attribute that has been calculated in
      the Initialize Context."
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: 'Mesh Sample:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 1417
      y: -899
      width: 323
      height: 139
    contents: 'We''re sampling the mesh to get the position and normal of the eye''s
      socket. We store that information in custom attributes to be able to use them
      later on.



      This allows us to avoid sampling the mesh in the update
      context, as it can be costly. Thanks to this, we''re sampling the mesh only
      when the particles are born.

'
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  categories: []
